  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The best strategy is to <b>Redeploy your environment using AWS CloudFormation, and utilize Autoscaling policies that scale dynamically based on incoming request rates and observed customer load times</b>. This approach uses CloudFormation for consistent provisioning and management, and leverages Auto Scaling to adjust EC2 instances automatically in response to increased traffic or slow load times, ensuring customers continue to experience quick page loads.</p><p>The other options have limitations:</p><ul><li><i>Redeploy your infrastructure using AWS CloudFormation, and set up a second CloudFormation stack. Use Elastic Load Balancer's spillover features to direct any overflow traffic to the additional stack.</i> – ELB does not natively support traffic spillover to another stack based on slow connections, and this adds unnecessary complexity.</li><li><i>Rebuild your app with an Auto Scaling template, configuring it to launch a new Elastic Beanstalk environment whenever customer load times exceed a preset threshold.</i> – Launching a new Elastic Beanstalk environment is slow and not intended for handling real-time scaling based on load. Auto Scaling groups within the current infrastructure would respond much faster to load changes.</li><li><i>Redeploy your environment with a CloudFormation template. Set up Elastic Load Balancer health checks to trigger the creation of a new CloudFormation stack when failures are detected.</i> – ELB health checks are designed for instance health and do not orchestrate deploying entire new stacks when latency increases, nor does creating new CloudFormation stacks address scaling needs directly.</li></ul><p><b>Pro tip:</b> Use Auto Scaling with metrics such as request count or latency for smooth scaling. Building your environment with CloudFormation ensures easy updates and reproducibility while keeping scaling logic efficient and cost-effective.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You manage a multi-tier Windows web app running on Amazon EC2 instances behind a load balancer. Metrics reveal increasing page load times for customers. Your manager asks you to prevent page load delays due to excessive incoming requests. Which is the most effective method to address this?</p>",
